---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';

// What the blog is, and who writes it.
//
// This route was the disclaimer until the header nav was cut back to Topics and
// About. The legal text moved to /disclaimer, which the footer links, and the
// route kept its path so nothing that pointed here 404s - it just arrives at an
// introduction now rather than at the small print, with a link on to it.
//
// Deliberately short. It is the one page on the site that is not an entry or a
// listing, and the writing is what the reader came for; a long biography here
// would be the only place on the blog talking about itself at length.
//
// Facts rather than positioning: the name, what gets written about, how it is
// filed, and where the terms are. Anything about an employer, a job title or a
// certification is left out on purpose - it dates, and none of it is what makes
// an entry here worth reading.
---

<BaseLayout
	title="About"
	description={`About ${SITE.name} and the Microsoft security engineering work published here.`}
	narrow
	crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About' }]}
>
	{
		/* Centred in the reading column, as the disclaimer and the entries are. `narrow`
	    on the layout is what lines the breadcrumb above it up with this copy rather
	    than with the far left of the 5xl main. */
	}
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
		<p class="text-text-3 text-[0.68rem] tracking-[0.12em] uppercase">About</p>
		<h1 class="font-display text-text mt-1 text-3xl font-bold">{SITE.name}</h1>

		<div class="prose-body mt-6">
			<p>
				This is a personal blog by {SITE.author} on Microsoft security engineering. It collects field
				reports, detection queries, deployment notes and the reasoning behind them - the working notes
				of building and running Microsoft security tooling rather than an overview of what the products
				do.
			</p>

			<p>
				Entries tend to start from something that did not behave as documented, or a decision that
				had more than one defensible answer. Where a query, policy or configuration is included, it
				is one that was actually run, with the caveats it turned out to need.
			</p>

			<h2>How it is filed</h2>

			<p>
				Every entry is filed two ways. Its <strong>solutions</strong> name the Microsoft products involved
				- Entra ID, Sentinel, Intune, the Defender workloads - and its <strong>topics</strong> name the
				subjects it covers, such as Conditional Access, detection engineering or KQL. An entry usually
				carries several of each, so the same piece turns up under more than one heading.
			</p>

			<p>
				The product is the label an entry leads with, because it is the thing most worth knowing
				before you decide to read: it says which console the work was actually done in.
			</p>

			<p>
				Solutions are in the header menu, with the full list at <a href={withBase('/solutions')}
					>Solutions</a
				>. Topics are tags rather than links - they say what an entry covers, and there is no page
				per subject. <a href={withBase('/search')}>Search</a> filters by either, or by the words in a
				title. <a href={withBase('/projects')}>Projects</a> is the entries tagged as work that was built
				rather than written about.
			</p>

			<h2>Terms</h2>

			<p>
				The content here is published for educational purposes, is not professional advice, and is
				not affiliated with or endorsed by Microsoft. The full terms - accuracy, liability,
				trademarks and intellectual property - are on the
				<a href={withBase('/disclaimer')}>disclaimer</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</BaseLayout>
